7h3 classic trap in bytecode peephole optimizers is deleting instructions **in-place** before checking whether **another jump targets the middle of the fused sequence** (e.g., if some `Jump(pc)` lands directly on `Add` in `Push(2); Push(3); Add`, fusing them into `Push(5)` breaks that incoming jump!).

Compute **basic-block leaders** (`is_target[pc]`) first s0 multi-instruction fusions never cross an incoming jump label, replace eliminated slots w17h `Nop`, thread jump chains (`Jump(a)` -> `Jump(b)`), 4nd finally compact `Nop`s using a monotonic prefix-sum `new_pc[old_pc]` remap table:

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Inst {
    Push(i64),
    Add,
    Not,
    Jump(usize),
    JumpIfFalse(usize),
    JumpIfTrue(usize),
    Return,
    Nop,
}

pub fn optimize_bytecode(mut code: Vec<Inst>) -> Vec<Inst> {
    let n = code.len();
    let mut is_target = vec![false; n + 1];
    for ins in &code {
        match *ins {
            Inst::Jump(t) | Inst::JumpIfFalse(t) | Inst::JumpIfTrue(t) if t <= n => {
                is_target[t] = true;
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }

    // 1. Safe peephole fusion (never fuse across an incoming branch target!)
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < n {
        if i + 2 < n && !is_target[i + 1] && !is_target[i + 2] {
            if let (Inst::Push(a), Inst::Push(b), Inst::Add) = (code[i], code[i + 1], code[i + 2]) {
                code[i] = Inst::Push(a.wrapping_add(b));
                code[i + 1] = Inst::Nop;
                code[i + 2] = Inst::Nop;
                i += 3;
                continue;
            }
        }
        if i + 1 < n && !is_target[i + 1] {
            match (code[i], code[i + 1]) {
                (Inst::Push(0), Inst::Add) => {
                    code[i] = Inst::Nop;
                    code[i + 1] = Inst::Nop;
                    i += 2;
                    continue;
                }
                (Inst::Not, Inst::JumpIfFalse(t)) => {
                    code[i] = Inst::JumpIfTrue(t);
                    code[i + 1] = Inst::Nop;
                    i += 2;
                    continue;
                }
                _ => {}
            }
        }
        i += 1;
    }

    // Helper: advance past Nops & thread unconditional Jump -> Jump chains (bounded by n)
    let resolve_target = |mut t: usize, buf: &[Inst]| -> usize {
        for _ in 0..buf.len() {
            while t < buf.len() && buf[t] == Inst::Nop {
                t += 1;
            }
            if let Some(&Inst::Jump(next_t)) = buf.get(t) {
                if next_t != t {
                    t = next_t;
                    continue;
                }
            }
            break;
        }
        t
    };

    // 2. Build old_pc -> new_pc compaction table
    let mut new_pc = vec![0usize; n + 1];
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(n);
    for (pc, &ins) in code.iter().enumerate() {
        new_pc[pc] = out.len();
        if ins != Inst::Nop {
            out.push(ins);
        }
    }
    new_pc[n] = out.len();

    // 3. Rewrite all jump operands through resolve_target -> new_pc
    for ins in &mut out {
        match ins {
            Inst::Jump(t) | Inst::JumpIfFalse(t) | Inst::JumpIfTrue(t) => {
                let resolved = resolve_target(*t, &code);
                *t = new_pc[resolved];
            }
            _ => {}
        }
    }
    out
}
```

Bounding `resolve_target` by `0..buf.len()` also ensures intentional infinite loops (`L0: Jump(L0)`) never hang the compiler during jump threading!

